39;ung\">Yu T'ung</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoto_(prince)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Yoto (prince)\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367954\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367954\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3678196</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 2 \u2014 Yoto</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">George A. Kennedy</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"935\" data-page-number=\"935\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/333\" data-page-index=\"333\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/333\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;333\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>YOTO</b> \u5cb3\u8a17, d. 1638, age 41 (<i>sui</i>), was a member of the Imperial Family and the eldest son of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dai%C5%A1an\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dai\u0161an\">Dai\u0161an</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. From 1621 to 1629 he was constantly engaged in fighting, and in 1626 received the rank of <i>beile</i> for his services in the expedition led by his father Dai\u0161an against the Mongolian tribe of Jarut. In the invasion of Korea in 1627 he was the fifth ranking <i>beile</i>, and after the surrender of the Korean king, Li Tsung (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Abahai\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Abahai\">Abahai</a></span>), he supported <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Jirgalang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Jirgalang\">Jirgalang's</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] arrangement of a peace treaty, as opposed to <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Amin\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Amin\">Amin's</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] project for further invasion. After taking an active part in the operations against the city of Ning-y\u00fcan, he returned in 1630 to be garrison commander of Sh\u00ean-yang. On the organization of the Six Ministries in the Manchu Administration in 1631, he was put in charge of the Ministry of War, and presented memorials to <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Abahai\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Abahai\">Abahai</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] urging conciliatory and constructive measures towards the conquered Chinese population. From 1632 to 1635 he accompanied the various expeditions against the Chahar Mongols, although prevented by sickness from playing an active part. In 1636 he received the title Ch'\u00eang Ch'in-wang \u6210\u89aa\u738b, but four months later was condemned to death for hiding the treacherous designs of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Mangg%C3%BBltai\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Mangg\u00fbltai\">Mangg\u00fbltai</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] and his own younger brother \u0160oto (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dorgon\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dorgon\">Dorgon</a></span>), and for instigating dissention between Jirgalang and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Haoge\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Haoge\">Haoge</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. Although pardoned, he was degraded to <i>beile</i> and in the following year to <i>beise</i> for another offense. Despite this he was, in 1638, put in command of the right wing of the army against the Ming troops and died in action at Ch'iang-tz\u016d-ling \u7246\u5b50\u5dba. His death was deeply mourned by Abahai who gave him posthumously the title K'o-ch'in Ch\u00fcn-wang \u514b\u52e4\u90e1\u738b. A memorial was erected in 1688 at his grave near Mukden, and in 1778, by order of Emperor Kao-tsung, a tablet to his honor was placed in the Imperial Ancestral Temple.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/222/9b; 2/3/1a; 3/8/16a; 34/123/1a.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">George A. Kennedy</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Abahai"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Amin"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Dai\u0161an"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Dorgon"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Haoge"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Jirgalang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Mangg\u00fbltai"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yolo"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yu T'ung"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:George A. Kennedy"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysec